{"id": "sevt_011dtE2u5FKih55dA7LPykvW", "input": {"evidence": "Source to sink:\n- app/routes/export.py:11-12: the route GET /export (export_report) has no authentication or authorization decorator in this file, so it looks reachable by any client that can reach the app.\n- app/routes/export.py:13: the attacker-controlled query parameter `sort` (request.args.get(\"sort\", \"id\")) goes through clean_identifier() from the external package acme_validation (imported at app/routes/export.py:3).\n- app/routes/export.py:15: the result is put straight into the SQL text with an f-string: f\"SELECT * FROM reports ORDER BY {column}\", then run with sqlite3 conn.execute. You can't use bound parameters for identifiers in ORDER BY, so the only control here is clean_identifier().\n- requirements.txt:2: the package is pinned as acme-validation==2.3.1. Its source is not in the repository, and I couldn't find it anywhere on the analysis filesystem (searched for acme_validation*). I can't tell whether clean_identifier() enforces a strict identifier allowlist or regex such as ^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*$, or whether it only strips or escapes some characters (for example quotes), which would still allow payloads like `id, (CASE WHEN (SELECT ...) THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)` or other boolean/time-based ORDER BY injection.\n- config.py:3: DB_PATH comes from the environment and is not attacker-influenced.\nConclusion: the sink is real and the input is attacker-controlled. Whether it can be exploited depends entirely on how clean_identifier() behaves, which I could not check. So I'm marking this needs_review rather than guessing.\nSide note (out of scope for F-107): config.py:4 contains a hardcoded cloud credential literal. I have not reproduced the value here. It should be triaged separately and rotated.", "finding_id": "F-107", "recommended_fix": "Don't depend on a generic sanitizer for SQL identifiers. Map the user value to an explicit server-side allowlist of sortable columns, e.g. ALLOWED = {\"id\": \"id\", \"created_at\": \"created_at\", \"name\": \"name\"}; column = ALLOWED.get(request.args.get(\"sort\", \"id\"), \"id\"), and reject or default anything else. Optionally quote the identifier after allowlisting. To close this ticket: get and review the source of acme-validation 2.3.1's clean_identifier() and confirm whether it is a strict allowlist/regex (not exploitable) or a character-stripping or escaping function (likely exploitable). Also confirm whether /export is protected by app-level auth that isn't in this repository.", "severity": "high", "title": "ORDER BY built with f-string from user-controlled ?sort= param; safety depends on unverified third-party clean_identifier()", "verdict": "needs_review"}, "name": "create_ticket_draft", "processed_at": "2026-10-26T20:55:58.333076Z", "type": "agent.custom_tool_use"}
