{"id": "sevt_01QaDjvDFS6qWKSUNJFgxpa2", "content": [{"text": "I triaged F-107 and created one ticket draft. The verdict is **needs_review**, because I couldn't tell whether this can actually be exploited.\n\n| ID | Rule | Location | Verdict | Severity | Key reason |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| F-107 | SQL Injection | app/routes/export.py:15 | needs_review | high | The `sort` query parameter is user input. It goes through `clean_identifier()` (from the `acme-validation==2.3.1` package) and then into an f-string `ORDER BY` clause. That package's code isn't in the repo or anywhere on the system, so I couldn't check whether it blocks injection. |\n\n**Path from input to query:** the `/export` route (app/routes/export.py:11), which has no login check in this file, reads `sort` and passes it to `clean_identifier()` (line 13). The result goes straight into the SQL text (line 15). Column names in `ORDER BY` can't be passed as safe query parameters, so `clean_identifier()` is the only protection.\n\n**Recommended fix:** replace `clean_identifier()` with a fixed list of allowed sort columns on the server, and reject or default anything else. To close the ticket, someone needs to review `clean_identifier()` in version 2.3.1 and confirm whether login is enforced elsewhere in the app.\n\n**Separate issue:** config.py:4 contains a hardcoded cloud credential. It isn't part of this finding, and I left the value out of the ticket. It should be rotated and tracked in its own ticket.", "type": "text"}], "processed_at": "2026-10-26T20:56:04.170191Z", "type": "agent.message"}
